\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl252\slmult1\i\f0\fs22 Waste Management Recycling Education Representative Bobby Bell provided an update \kerning2 on upcoming free community events, including the final composting class of the year at Polliwog Park and the last paper-shredding event of the year at the Mira Costa High School student parking lot. Residents and local business owners may bring up to four banker boxes for shredding.\kerning0\line\line Parks and Recreation Supervisor David Ibarra invited the community \kerning2 to the 36th Annual Pumpkin Race\kerning0  at the Manhattan Beach Pier on Sunday, October 18, 2026, from 12:00 PM - 6:00 PM\kerning2  and recognized event sponsors. He noted participation highlights from the prior year and announced that pumpkin kits are available for purchase at City Hall and local businesses. He also informed the \kerning0 community \kerning2 of the upcoming Family Halloween Carnival at Manhattan Heights\kerning0  Park on Saturday, October 24, 2026\kerning2 , \kerning0 from 1:00 PM - 5:00 PM \kerning2 featuring games, activities, a haunted house, and costume contests.\kerning0\line\line Cultural Arts Supervisor Eric Brinkman invited the community \kerning2 to \kerning0 the Public Art Master Plan Open House Mixer\kerning2  on\kerning0  at the Manhattan Beach Library on Friday,\kerning2  October 9\kerning0 , 2026, from 2:00 PM - 6:00 PM\kerning2  to support the revision of the City\rquote s Public Art Master Plan.\kerning0  \kerning2 The event will provide information about the plan, examples of public art, interactive activities, and opportunities for residents to share input with the project team.\kerning0\line\line Fire Engineer Joshua Kueng, \kerning2 on behalf of the Manhattan Beach Firefighters Association, invited the community to the 4th Annual Pancake Breakfast at Fire Station 1\kerning0  \kerning2 on October 11\kerning0 , 2026, from 8:00 AM - 12:00 PM\kerning2 . He noted that the event includes a family-friendly breakfast, fire engine tours, and recognition of second-grade winners of the Exit Drill in the Home program. Proceeds will benefit the Manhattan Beach Firefighters Association and the Manhattan Beach Firefighters Burn Foundation.\kerning0\line\line Vice Chair Hildy Stern, on behalf of the South Bay Coalition Against Hate, \kerning2 thanked the City for recognizing United Against Hate Week and announced the Coalition\rquote s upcoming programming beginning October 19\kerning0 , 2026\kerning2 . Activities include community yoga events, a civility discussion panel, El Segundo\rquote s cultural celebration, and a \lquote Wear Yellow\rquote  day to promote unity. She also noted that a two-week regional \kerning0 canned \kerning2 food drive will take place to support the Nutrition Pantry at the Richstone Family Center, with multiple collection sites throughout the South Bay.\kerning0\i0\f1\line\line\i\f0 Library Manager Cassandra Sargent invited the community \kerning2 to a murder mystery event at the library, held in anticipation of the upcoming Michael Connelly author event. She noted that the program will include interactive sleuthing activities, prizes, and themed references for fans.\kerning0\line\line Police Chief Rachel Johnson invited the community to two \kerning2 upcoming events: a Police Department Town Hall on October 21\kerning0 , 2026, from 6:00 PM - 8:00 PM\kerning2  at the Jo\kerning0 slyn Community\kerning2  Center, which will include information on El Ni\'f1o preparedness and an opportunity for residents to submit questions in advance, and the Trunk or Treat event in the Upper Civic Center lot\kerning0  \kerning2 on October 29\kerning0 , 2026\kerning2 ,\kerning0  from 5:00 PM - 7:00 PM\kerning2  featuring candy, games, and opportunities to meet City department\kerning0 s.\line\line Councilmember Howorth \kerning2 reported on two recent community events: the car show benefiting the Police Department\rquote s K-9 Foundation, which was well attended, and the annual Hometown Fair, which experienced lower attendance due to extreme heat. She acknowledged the efforts of Police, Fire, and City staff in providing support and ensuring public safety throughout the fair, noting that protocols and metrics were in place to assess weather-related health risks. She encouraged continued community support for local organizations whose fundraising was impacted.\kerning0\i0\f1\line\line\kerning2\i\f0 Mayor Pro Tem Tarnay provided an update on mosquito activity and the prevention of West Nile virus, noting the increased risk due to warm weather. She encouraged residents to eliminate standing water around their homes, maintain pools and spas, change water in bowls and bird baths regularly, and use insect repellent when outdoors. She also shared contact information for the Los Angeles County West Vector Control District for residents seeking assistance or inspections.\kerning0  Their phone number is 310-915-7370.\line\line\pard\kerning2 Mayor Franklin commented on the success of the 10K race associated with the Hometown Fair, noting the strong participation and thanking the 10K Committee for their work. He also encouraged early registration for future races due to high demand and echoed appreciation for the Hometown Fair, encouraging continued community support for the organizations that rely on it.\cf1\kerning0\i0\f2\fs24\par
